    {
      "id": "p0204-b009",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "source": {
        "text": "Bratty and Catty are unique in that both start speaking and stop speaking at the same time. Toby noted that some of these simultaneous lines might cause the game to \"mess up\" unless Bratty and Catty use an equal number of letters when speaking. Naturally, the translation process demolished this synchronization, so Bratty and Catty's lines required extra fine-tuning to display properly in Japanese.",
        "raw": "Bratty and Catty are unique in that both start speaking and stop speaking at\nthe same time. Toby noted that some of these simultaneous lines might\ncause the game to \"mess up\" unless Bratty and Catty use an equal number\nof letters when speaking. Naturally, the translation process demolished this\nsynchronization, so Bratty and Catty's lines required extra fine-tuning to\ndisplay properly in Japanese.",
        "status": "corrected",
        "languages": [
          "en"
        ],
        "bbox": [
          0.29324088,
          0.80010714,
          0.86707483,
          0.90402143
        ],
        "mask_bbox": [
          0.2874541,
          0.79939286,
          0.89640398,
          0.90509286
        ],
        "line_ids": [
          68,
          71,
          73,
          75,
          77,
          79
        ]
      },
      "translation": {
        "text": "她们独特之处在于，总是同时开始说话，也同时停下。Toby 指出，有些同步台词必须让两人的字母数相等，否则游戏可能“出乱子”。翻译自然会打乱这种同步，所以她们的日语台词还得格外细调，才能正常显示。",
        "status": "draft",
        "spans": []
